
AUDIT TRAIL
From Attack Vectors to Audit Alignment Framework
A documented record of auditor-driven corrections to Voice Protest's public defence reference, June 2026

This document exists to make the evolution of Voice Protest's canonical defence reference traceable: what was said before, what an external technical and epistemological audit flagged, what changed, and why. It covers three successive documents and one pending revision.

Documents in this trail
—	Document A — "Attack Vectors & Responses" (internal, June 2026). Tactical, defensive, organised as attack/response pairs.
—	Document B — Auditor's review notes (June 2026). A structured set of corrections and additions delivered in prose, not yet integrated.
—	Document C — "Audit Alignment, Threat Model and Participation Framework" v2.0 (June 2026). First integration attempt, restructured around a foundational principle.
—	Document D — Auditor's second-pass review of Document C (June 2026). Confirms direction but withholds canonical status pending further terminology cleanup.
 
1. Why Document A was revised
Document A ("Attack Vectors & Responses") was written as an internal tactical playbook: thirteen specific attacks, each with a "technical reality" and a ready-to-publish "Public response" quote. It served its purpose for rapid internal preparation, but the auditor's review (Document B) identified that its register — defensive, quote-ready, organised around refuting specific accusations — was structurally unsuited to becoming the platform's canonical reference. A document built to win arguments is not the same as a document built to state limits accurately.
The auditor's stated criteria for the revision were twofold: that every claim be demonstrably true of the deployed code, and that every claim be defensible in front of a hostile auditor, a critical journalist, or a sceptical academic. Four specific corrections and four new sections were required to meet that bar.
2. Corrections to existing language
The auditor identified four places where Document A's language overstated what the platform actually verifies or could credibly claim.
2.1 — Citizenship language
BEFORE (DOCUMENT A)
"citizens", "citizenship", "verified citizens", "national citizens" — used throughout Category 2 (geographic eligibility) to describe who is eligible to participate.
AFTER (AUDITOR'S CORRECTION)
"verified participants", "participants linked to the relevant community", "nationally affiliated participants".
Reason: Voice Protest verifies SIM affiliation or institutional email membership — not legal citizenship. The platform has no access to civil registries and cannot make a legal citizenship determination. Using "citizenship" language implies a legal claim the system cannot support.

2.2 — Criminal language
BEFORE (DOCUMENT A)
"criminal offence", "illegal activity", "criminal fraud" — used when describing SIM farming and large-scale manipulation attempts.
AFTER (AUDITOR'S CORRECTION)
"may violate local laws or regulations", "may breach terms of service".
Reason: Voice Protest is not a judicial authority and cannot characterise conduct as criminal. That determination belongs to courts and law enforcement in the relevant jurisdiction.

2.3 — Jurisdiction-specific SIM regulation claims
BEFORE (DOCUMENT A)
"In the EU, SIM registration laws since 2023 require ID verification in most countries."
AFTER (AUDITOR'S CORRECTION)
"The cost and traceability of obtaining large numbers of SIM cards varies by jurisdiction, but is generally higher than creating email accounts or petition signatures."
Reason: Specific dates and jurisdiction counts age quickly and are easy for a critical reader to fact-check and disprove in any single counter-example. The corrected language makes the same point — that SIM acquisition has a real cost — without a falsifiable factual claim.

2.4 — Absolute auditability claims
BEFORE (DOCUMENT A)
"Any auditor would detect manipulation" (implied across Category 1 and Category 3 responses).
AFTER (AUDITOR'S CORRECTION)
"Manipulation attempts would be subject to independent audit, integrity verification and consistency checks."
Reason: The original framing claims a guaranteed detection outcome. The corrected framing accurately describes that mechanisms for detection exist, without promising they will always succeed.
 
3. New sections added
Beyond correcting existing language, the auditor identified four gaps — claims the document needed to make explicit rather than leave implied or unaddressed.
3.1 — Scope of Measurement
Document A had no direct response to the attack "Voice Protest only measures its own users." The auditor's addition states plainly that Voice Protest does not measure society — it measures verified participation among people who voluntarily choose to participate, a limitation explicit and inherent to demonstrations, petitions, referenda and surveys alike. The platform makes no claim to represent non-participants.
3.2 — What Voice Protest Does Not Measure
A companion to the platform's existing data-inventory transparency (what is stored / not stored), but applied to measurement rather than storage. Voice Protest measures verified participation, participation growth, and participation distribution. It explicitly does not measure public opinion, silent support, silent opposition, legitimacy, or electoral preference. Any interpretation beyond verified participation belongs to external observers, not the platform.
3.3 — Permanent Records
Responds directly to the attack "Voice Protest creates a permanent database of dissent." The corrected answer separates what is permanently preserved (public protest reports, integrity commitments, integrity hashes) from what is never preserved (participant identities, phone numbers, personal records), framing the system's design intent as preserving evidence of participation while avoiding preservation of participant identity.
3.4 — Institutional Recognition
Responds to "the institution does not recognise this protest." Voice Protest does not require institutional recognition; the platform records verified participation, and recognition, response and engagement remain entirely at the discretion of the institution concerned.
 
4. The elevated principle
The auditor singled out one sentence already present in Document A's key-messages list and recommended elevating it from a communications talking point to the document's organising principle:
“We verify participation, not truth.”
Voice Protest verifies that a participation event occurred. It does not verify whether the underlying claim is true.

This became the Foundational Principle section opening Document C. The auditor's reasoning: this is the single strongest conceptual defence available to the platform, and every other section — what is measured, what is not measured, representativeness, reliability scores — follows logically from it once stated first.
4.1 — Representativeness (added on the same logic)
A direct consequence of the foundational principle, added as its own section: Voice Protest is not a referendum. Referenda measure the preferences of an entire eligible population; Voice Protest measures verified participation among those who voluntarily choose to express support for a specific grievance. The two instruments serve different purposes. The auditor noted this section specifically protects the platform against comparisons to Swiss-style referenda, national elections, or official consultations — comparisons a critical reader reaches for naturally and which the platform cannot win on those terms, nor needs to.
5. Deliberately deferred: the research vocabulary
The auditor was explicit that exploratory research framing — terms such as Social Seismology, Democratic Thermodynamics, Democratic Digital Twin, VCP, IPE, or CVWS — should not appear in this document at all, regardless of their conceptual merit elsewhere.
The stated reason: this document is a defence of the existing system, not a manifesto for future research. Mixing the two registers would let a critical reader dismiss the platform's current, demonstrable claims by association with speculative future ones. The single concession is a short closing section:
—	Future Research Directions — states only that Voice Protest is currently focused on verified participation, and that future research may explore whether aggregated participation data can contribute to broader studies of democratic observability, institutional responsiveness and collective civic behaviour. Explicitly flagged as exploratory and not to be confused with current platform capabilities.
6. Document C: the first integration
Document C ("Audit Alignment, Threat Model and Participation Framework", v2.0) is the result of integrating the corrections in Sections 2 and 3 above. It restructures the material entirely: where Document A organised itself as attack/response pairs across five categories, Document C opens with the foundational principle and lets every subsequent section — what is measured, what is not measured, scope of measurement, representativeness, reliability scores, threat model — follow from it. The thirteen specific attacks in Document A's Category 1–5 are condensed into seven abstract attack categories, and the "Public response" quote format is replaced by declarative statements.
This was a substantial improvement and incorporated the corrections above faithfully.
7. Document D: why v2.0 is still not canonical
The auditor's second-pass review (Document D) confirms that Document C correctly applies the structural and conceptual corrections — the foundational principle, the new sections, the softened legal and citizenship language are all present and well-integrated. The assessment nonetheless withholds canonical status, for a specific reason rather than a general one: Document C still carries residual phrasing inherited from earlier drafting passes that has not yet been checked line-by-line against four references that have moved since those passages were first written:
—	the completed documentary audit;
—	the current README;
—	the “Verified Collective Voice” repositioning;
—	the terminology cleanup on citizenship language documented in Section 2.1 above.
The auditor's explicit recommendation is not a partial patch but an integral review: read Document C end to end against those four references and correct any remaining drift, rather than spot-fixing individual sentences as they are noticed. The auditor noted this as the single most important remaining step, describing the underlying improvement still needed as epistemological rather than technical — ensuring it is stated unambiguously, throughout, that Voice Protest measures verified participation and nothing else: not legitimacy, not public opinion, not truth.

STATUS AS OF THIS DOCUMENT
Document C (v2.0) is the current working reference and is materially correct. It is not yet formally canonical pending the integral cross-check described above. No further new sections are required — the remaining work is verification of existing language against the four references, not additional drafting.
7. Session corrections — 21 June 2026
This entry documents technical corrections, new features and documentation updates applied during the development session of 21 June 2026.
7.1 — New scope: Local (city/municipality)
A fourth geographic scope, “local”, was added to the platform. Unlike the existing regional scope, the local scope requires the event creator to select a specific municipality via Nominatim/OpenStreetMap search. The municipality is identified by its OSM relation ID (admin_level=8), eliminating name ambiguity across languages. This approach was validated for all 27 EU member states. GPS confirmation from participants is used to classify adhesions into three tiers in the public report: GPS confirmed within the declared municipality, national participants without local GPS, and international participants. GPS is not a participation requirement — it is a verification signal. The design decision not to exclude non-GPS participants was deliberate: Voice Protest verifies participation, not legitimacy. A resident temporarily absent from the municipality retains the right to participate.
7.2 — Scope “regional” renamed from “Local”
The existing scope previously labelled “Local” in the user interface was renamed “Regional” across all four languages (ES/EN/FR/ZH) to avoid confusion with the new local city/municipality scope. The technical scope identifier (“regional”) was unchanged. Eligibility and duration (8h) were unchanged.
7.3 — Event start time timezone correction
Prior to this session, all event start times were encoded as 08:00 UTC regardless of the event creator’s location. This meant that an event created in Santander (CEST, UTC+2) would start at 10:00 local time, not 08:00. The bug was corrected: start times are now encoded with the local timezone offset of the creator’s browser at creation time, so 08:00 always means 08:00 in the creator’s city.
7.4 — Push notification timezone and night filter
The device timezone (IANA string) is now captured at push subscription time and stored in the push_subscriptions table. The “closing soon” notification (sent one hour before event closure) is suppressed for participants whose device timezone indicates nighttime hours (23:00–07:00 local time) at the moment of sending. The “started” and “closed” notifications are not filtered. This change was reviewed by the external auditor, who approved it as technically sound and within the platform’s existing privacy model. The Privacy Policy was updated in all four languages to disclose timezone storage.
7.5 — tipo_abuso values aligned to English
The frontend select options for tipo_abuso were using Spanish values (opacidad, corrupcion, negligencia…) while the backend VALID_ABUSE_TYPES list expected English values (opacity, corruption, negligence…). This mismatch caused all protest creation attempts to fail with a 400 validation error. The frontend values were corrected to match the backend. Display labels shown to users are unchanged — only the internal values sent to the API were corrected.
7.6 — Source domain classification fix and new regional media
The classifyDomain function now checks both the full domain and its registrable domain (eTLD+1), so subdomains (e.g. eldiariocantabria.publico.es) inherit their parent domain’s classification. Several Spanish regional media outlets were added to the KNOWN_OFFICIAL list: eldiariomontanes.es, elcorreo.com, diariovasco.com, heraldo.es and others.
7.7 — Wikidata ALLOWED_TYPES expanded
Three Wikidata types were added to the allowed recipients list: Q22996476 (ayuntamiento — Spanish local government institution), Q543654 (city council, generic), and Q2074737 (municipality of Spain). These additions were required to allow the Ayuntamiento de Santander (Q5714934) to be accepted as a valid recipient.
Session corrections — 24 June 2026
This entry documents technical corrections, an interface restructuring and a methodological refinement applied during the development session of 24 June 2026.
Regional scope — duplicate region field removed
The event-creation screen displayed two “Region/Province” fields for the regional scope: a legacy free-text input and the newer OpenStreetMap search added on 21 June. The free-text field was removed. The region name is now derived from the OSM selection and kept in sync, so the group screen and group invitations continue to display it. The redundant region validation was removed; the requirement to select a region from the OSM search now governs region entry.
Institutional participation presented as a distinct model
The scope selector was reorganised into two clearly separated blocks. The first, geographic scope, groups National, Regional, Local and Global. The second, institutional scope, groups two institutional participation types: “Institutional — email domain” and “Institutional + trusted census”. Institutional participation is no longer hidden as an advanced option inside the regional scope. Internally it still reuses existing backend fields — regional scope plus an institutional email domain, and, for the census variant, the trusted-census flag — so no new backend scope was introduced. Because institutional convocatorias are verified by institutional email rather than geography, they are exempt from the requirement to declare an OSM geographic entity. On the participant side, the SIM/GPS geographic-validation panel is hidden for institutional convocatorias, since these are verified by email. The scope descriptions were rewritten in all four languages (ES/EN/FR/ZH) to match this two-block structure.
Event start date — out-of-range and daylight-saving corrections
Two defects in the event start time were corrected. First, the end-date preview could render an absurd value (for example, the year 6) when the browser’s native date input briefly held an incomplete year; guards now hide the preview and reject submission for any start date earlier than the minimum allowed date. Second, the “08:00 local” start time was being computed using the creator’s timezone offset on the day of creation rather than on the event date, producing a one-hour error across daylight-saving boundaries. Start times are now constructed from the event date’s own local components, so 08:00 always means 08:00 local on that date regardless of season.
Geographic confidence — physical presence weighted above device configuration
The device geographic-confidence score was revised so that agreement between IP and GPS is the dominant signal, while timezone and language are treated as auxiliary and compared against the GPS country rather than the IP country. With GPS present, the score is 60 (GPS) + 15 (IP present) + 20 (GPS country equals IP country) + 10 (timezone equals GPS country) + 5 (language equals GPS country). The branch without GPS is unchanged. The practical effect is that a participant physically present in a country while using a foreign-configured device — for example a Spanish-configured phone in Malta, with both IP and GPS in Malta — is no longer penalised: the score rises from 85% to 95%. This reflects the platform’s position that coinciding IP and GPS are a stronger signal of physical presence than timezone or language, which describe device configuration rather than location.
National eligibility decoupled from geographic confidence
The geographic-confidence score is no longer used as an eligibility condition. National eligibility is determined solely by the SIM country matching the convocatoria’s country. The previous “confidence at least 60%” requirement was removed from the adhesion check, so that legitimate participants whose auxiliary signals are weak or misaligned — a failing IP lookup, disabled GPS, a misaligned timezone — are not excluded. Geographic confidence is now strictly informative, explaining the quality of complementary signals without acting as a barrier.
Territorial verification exposed as local_verified
Territorial verification is now a named, explicit field, kept separate from the confidence score, in both the adhesion response and the public report. The field local_verified answers a single question: did the participant’s reverse-geocoded GPS place them inside the convocatoria’s declared OSM entity? It is accompanied by geo_scope_match (municipality or region) and geo_scope_source (gps_osm). This separation is deliberate: a confidence percentage measures signal quality, whereas local_verified measures territorial belonging, and the two must not be conflated by participants, journalists or future auditors. Consistent with the platform’s principle, local and regional participation remains a classification in the report, never an exclusion.
GPS coordinates — storage misalignment corrected
During this session’s documentation review, the auditor identified that the post-adhesion GPS reinforcement endpoint attempted to write GPS coordinates — latitude, longitude and accuracy — to the adhesions table. This contradicted the platform’s stated philosophy and the migration of 7 June 2026, which had deliberately removed those columns so that GPS coordinates are never stored: only a boolean confirming that GPS was active at the moment of adhesion, together with the derived OSM entity, is retained. Because the columns no longer exist and the update did not check for errors, the write failed silently — the reinforcement upgraded nothing. The endpoint was corrected to persist only the gps_confirmed boolean, the derived OSM identifier and the reliability signals, and error checking was added so that a future failure of this kind cannot pass unnoticed. The correction realigns the code with the database, the Privacy Policy and the canonical reference, all of which already state that GPS coordinates are never stored; no documentation change was required, because that claim is now true again.
Identity hashing consolidated server-side with HMAC
A review of the data-handling guarantees revealed that identity hashing was being performed in the browser using plain SHA-256 for both phone numbers (at sign-in) and institutional emails (in the trusted-census flow), with the resulting hash sent to and stored by the server. Because a browser cannot hold a server secret, a client-side hash can only ever be plain SHA-256 — which, for low-entropy identifiers such as phone numbers and predictable institutional email addresses, is recoverable by dictionary attack. This contradicted the platform’s own cryptographic principle, under which low-entropy identifiers must be protected with a keyed HMAC. The server-side HMAC helper existed but was applied only to a secondary device record, not to the identifier actually stored against each adhesion. Separately, the trusted-census vouching flow persisted candidate email addresses in plain text.
Identity hashing was consolidated entirely on the server. The raw phone number or email travels to the backend only for one-time verification, exactly as before; the backend computes the HMAC with a server-side secret and returns the resulting identifier; the client stores and reuses that value but never derives an identity hash itself. A single secret is reused for phone and email, since it is conceptually an identity-hash secret rather than a phone-specific one. The per-adhesion nullifier is derived as a keyed HMAC of the identity hash and the convocatoria identifier, so it differs across convocatorias and cannot be correlated. Institutional and census participation now resolve to the same HMAC for a given email, restoring their consistency. In the vouching flow the candidate email is masked (for example j***@uu.nl) and the raw address is never persisted. The server now refuses to start in production if the identity-hashing secrets are absent, rather than silently downgrading to plain SHA-256, and the browser-side hashing helpers — together with a misleading live “SHA-256” preview shown during sign-in — were removed.
Session corrections — 28 June 2026
This entry documents a database security review, two data-retention changes, a methodological interface-language audit, and the removal of two unimplemented login options, applied during the development session of 28 June 2026.
Row Level Security enabled on all remaining tables
A review of the Supabase database confirmed, by direct query against pg_tables, that Row Level Security was enabled only on the protests table (corrected in the 20 June 2026 migration) and was still disabled on every other table in the public schema. Although the frontend holds no Supabase client and all data flows through the Railway backend with the service-role key, the public anonymous key combined with PostgREST remained an attack surface independent of the frontend. Row Level Security was enabled, each with a service-role-only policy, on the fourteen remaining tables: groups, group_members, vouches, vouch_requests, invite_links, institutional_members, email_otp_requests, email_otp_rate_limit, platform_fund, financial_movements, kofi_protest_map, integrity_records, source_validations and admin_log. A repeat of the verification query now returns no tables with Row Level Security disabled. The migration is idempotent and safe to re-run.
Soft-delete replaced by irreversible anonymisation at 90 days
The previous data-retention mechanism logically deleted individual adhesions 90 days after an event closed by setting a deleted_at marker, while leaving the underlying personal fields intact in the database as a deliberate safety net for disputes and audits. The review concluded that retaining personal data indefinitely behind a soft-delete flag was inconsistent with the platform’s minimisation principle. The mechanism was replaced by irreversible anonymisation: 90 days after closure, every individual or linkable field of an adhesion — phone hash, device identifier, nullifier, public commitment, fine-grained location, city and region — is permanently set to null, while the row itself is kept so that the closed public report retains its aggregate distribution. The auditable snapshot in integrity_records, which contains no personal data, is unaffected. A safety guard ensures a protest is only anonymised once its integrity record exists. The auditor reviewed and approved the change.
Verified-device retention introduced (270 days of inactivity)
The devices table associates a random device identifier with a server-side HMAC pseudonym of the participant’s phone number. This association lets returning participants join later events without repeating SMS verification, and prevents duplicate participation. The review noted that this pseudonym was retained indefinitely by default, including for devices that verified once and never participated again — a persistent, correlatable identifier outliving any activity. A retention rule was introduced: a last_seen column, refreshed on each effective adhesion, now governs a 270-day inactivity window (three times the 90-day adhesion window). A daily job deletes any device with no adhesion for 270 days that is no longer referenced by an adhesion; because anonymised adhesions hold a null device identifier, an eligible device has no referencing rows and is removed safely. The window is sliding: a recurring participant never expires, while a device that returns after deletion simply re-verifies once by SMS. The privacy policy was updated in all four languages to declare this persistent identifier and its retention honestly, including the fact that, for a continuously active participant, the pseudonym persists for as long as participation continues.
Interface-language audit across four locales
A methodological audit of the user-facing strings was conducted against a single principle: the interface must not overstate what the system verifies. Across the four locales (ES/EN/FR/ZH), wording that conflated evidence with conclusion, confidence with certainty, or participation with legitimacy was corrected. Geographic signals are now described as confidence evidence rather than as certifying location; institutional email verification is described as evidence supporting affiliation rather than proving it; a declared source is described as supporting the reported facts rather than proving them; and “proof of humanity” became “humanity signal”. The data-retention wording was aligned with the new anonymisation and device-retention rules. Key sets were kept identical across the four languages, and the Chinese strings were flagged for native-speaker review before the pilot.
Unimplemented login options removed (World ID and high-risk mode)
The login screen’s advanced options presented two cards that, on tap, displayed only a “coming soon” notice. Neither had any implementation, dependency or backend logic. The World ID card additionally carried a “maximum verifiable privacy” badge, advertising as available a feature that did not exist. The high-risk-mode card was misleading in the opposite direction: it presented as a future feature a protection that is, in fact, already implemented and automatic — for events marked high or critical risk, the code already skips the GPS request entirely, so no location is collected, and the protest’s risk level is shown on its detail screen. Both cards, and the now-empty advanced-options accordion, were removed, and the nine orphaned interface strings were deleted from all four locales. The real, automatic high-risk protection is unchanged.
Session corrections — July 2026
This entry documents a set of changes applied during the development sessions of early July 2026: a change to how recipient institutions are validated, a broadening of the accepted abuse types, a new requirement for institutional events, and a redesign of the landing page around the map.
Recipient validation moved to hierarchical Wikidata classification
The server-side recipient check (16.4) previously matched a recipient’s declared Wikidata type against a flat list of accepted type identifiers. That list required a new manual entry for every institution type it did not yet cover — the additions logged in 7.7 (a Spanish city council, among others) were an example — and could never be complete. The check now traverses the Wikidata subclass hierarchy (instance-of, then subclass-of transitively) against a small set of public-authority classes, so a specific national institution type is recognised through its parent classes. An allowed public classification takes precedence over a rejected one, so a public enterprise is admitted while a private company or a political party is rejected; verdicts are cached. This supersedes the manual list-maintenance approach recorded in 7.7.
Accepted abuse types broadened from seven to sixteen
The closed list of accepted abuse types (16.1) was broadened from seven categories to sixteen, grouped in three families anchored to recognised frameworks: integrity and corruption (UN Convention against Corruption), maladministration (European Ombudsman and Article 41 of the EU Charter), and rights and liberties (ECHR and EU Charter), plus a residual ‘other public power abuse’. The frontend selector and the backend closed list are kept in exact synchronisation. The change widens the range of legitimate public grievances the platform can express without weakening the other three admission rules.
Institutional country now mandatory
Institutional convocatorias now declare the institution’s country as a mandatory field. Eligibility is unchanged — it remains membership of the approved email domain — but the declared country records the institution’s seat so the convocatoria can be placed on the map and reached through the new country-first navigation. This updates 20.6, which previously stated that institutional convocatorias declare no geographic entity; the country is a locator for discoverability only and produces no geographic breakdown of participants.
Landing redesigned around country-first map navigation
The landing page was reorganised so the map is the primary way to browse. On load it frames the user’s country — using the strongest signal available without a permission prompt (SIM country if known, otherwise IP country), and refining to the SIM country if it arrives later and the user has not moved the map — leaving neighbouring countries visible. Tapping a country recentres the map and filters the list to that country. The scope filter order was aligned to global, national, regional, local and institutional, matching the event-creation screen; institutional convocatorias are now shown as their own category, distinct from regional, in both the list and the map.
Country names localised; service worker made deploy-safe
Two smaller corrections. Country names in the convocatoria list are now localised to the interface language at render time, using the browser’s own Intl data, instead of a fixed string stored at creation. And the service worker was switched to a network-first strategy for the application shell, so a new deployment is picked up on the next load instead of after a stale-cache delay.
8. Why this audit trail exists
Voice Protest is an experimental civic infrastructure project. Because both the technology and the conceptual framework continue to evolve, maintaining a transparent record of corrections is considered a governance feature rather than a weakness.
The objective of this document is not to demonstrate that the project was always correct. The objective is to demonstrate that identified inaccuracies, overstatements and ambiguities are corrected openly and traceably.

This document is a historical record, not a description of the system.
It should be preserved and cited as governance history — evidence of how the platform's canonical reference was corrected over time — but never cited as a current description of what Voice Protest is or does. That role belongs exclusively to the Audit Alignment, Threat Model and Participation Framework, in its latest version.
 

