Session corrections — 24 July 2026 — GPS reinforcement writing stale IP-derived city/region
Found via a real test case (a participant joined without GPS from Santander, then confirmed location on the post-adhesion reinforcement card): the informe showed GPS confirmed alongside “Hernani, País Vasco” — a city the participant had never been near.
Cause: the reinforcement endpoint (PATCH /:id/adhesion) correctly recalculated gps_confirmed and adhesion_osm_id from the fresh GPS coordinates, but left a comment explicitly deciding not to update ciudad/region/pais, on the stated assumption “they were set correctly at join time from IP” — an assumption the Methodology’s own §4 already documented as false for mobile-carrier IP geolocation. Fixed: the reinforcement endpoint now updates ciudad/region/pais/pais_code from the same fresh reverse-geocode it already performs, guarded so a failed geocode never overwrites good data with nulls.
A second, related real-world test (a second participant, GPS genuinely disabled on their device) confirmed the system’s correct behaviour in the negative case: senales correctly omitted “gps”, fiabilidad correctly reflected IP-only evidence (85%), and the report correctly showed the participant as “nacional sin GPS” rather than fabricating a location.
Following from this, and from a broader review of IP geolocation reliability (international, not Spain-specific — mobile-carrier CGNAT routing produces the same city/region unreliability everywhere, confirmed by independent studies and by a live test of the founder’s own home broadband connection, off by roughly 10km even on a fixed line): IP-derived ciudad/region were removed from the location-resolution service entirely. IP still contributes country (pais/pais_code), which remains the only classification — national/international — that has ever depended on it; without GPS, ciudad/region are now left null rather than populated with an estimate the Methodology itself says cannot be trusted at that granularity.
Documents updated in this session: none directly; this entry is itself the record, and the informe’s own caveat text (“estimación aproximada por IP”) no longer has anything to caveat, since there is no city-level IP estimate left to show.
