Session corrections — 9 August 2026 — Closure mechanism silently broken since 10–11 June 2026
Discovered while wiring IndexNow to convocatoria closure — investigating why no such event yet existed surfaced that it did exist, and had been failing every single time it ran, since the day it was written.
The scheduled job auto-close-protests (pg_cron, every 30 minutes, added 11 June 2026) filtered and wrote a status column on protests — a column that has never existed on this table, confirmed against the original schema and every migration since. Every run failed silently inside pg_cron (logged only to cron.job_run_details, nowhere the application would surface it). calculate_integrity_hash_v2() (also 11 June 2026) separately selected a column named cities_count — the real column has always been named cities. Even had the scheduled job worked, this function would have failed too.
The practical result: Principle 7 (“At the closure of each convocation, Voice Protest computes a cryptographic integrity hash...”) had never actually executed in production. Every closed convocatoria’s hash_integridad was empty, including real ones (Las Llamas, closed 31 July, still unclosed as of this investigation).
Fixed: the closure job no longer depends on any status column — it uses ends_at < NOW() AND hash_integridad IS NULL as both the “needs closing” filter and its own idempotency guard. calculate_integrity_hash_v2() reads cities. A one-time catch-up closed every convocatoria that should already have closed.
A second, unrelated schema mismatch was found the same day testing the fix: the closure job’s surplus-transfer step assumed platform_fund was a ledger table (type, amount, source, protest_id, description) — its own creation migration (10 June 2026) says as much — but the real table in production (confirmed via information_schema.columns) is a single running balance (id, currency, balance, updated_at), stuck at 0.00 since the day it was created. The migration that supposedly created the ledger design was evidently never applied as written. Because every step of the closure job runs as one atomic statement, this one column-mismatch error rolled back the entire closure — hash included — for any convocatoria with a positive balance, for every run since the first one to hit it (31 July, Las Llamas). Fixed to match the real single-balance design; a second catch-up closed the backlog.
