Session corrections — 9 August 2026 — Public integrity verification confirmed working end-to-end for the first time
With closure now genuinely functioning, the first live end-to-end test of the “Verificar integridad” control (present in the report UI since 18 July 2026, per that entry) was run against a real closed convocatoria. It failed — not silently this time, but with a real “mismatch” result, on data nobody had touched.
Two further bugs, found via this single test. First: routes/public.js’s /protests/:id/integrity-data endpoint — the public data source the browser reads to independently recompute the hash — selected the same non-existent cities_count column found in the entry above, in four separate places. The endpoint had been returning an error on every call since it was written; the client-side verification control had never actually completed a comparison before this session. Fixed to select and return cities.
Second, with that fixed, verification still failed. Cause: calculate_integrity_hash_v2() casts the first/last adhesion timestamps with ::TEXT when building the hash input — Postgres’s own default cast (space-separated, no “T”). What gets stored in integrity_records.first_adhesion/last_adhesion, though, is the raw TIMESTAMPTZ value, which PostgREST later re-serialises to JSON as ISO 8601 (with “T”) when the public endpoint reads it back out. Two different strings for the same instant, so client-side recomputation could never match the server-computed hash — a false-negative, not a security defect; nothing was ever actually forgeable. Fixed by storing the exact text used in the hash (new columns, first_adhesion_text/last_adhesion_text) and having the public endpoint return that stored text as-is, rather than two independent code paths each re-deriving a timestamp string that was never guaranteed to agree.
A retroactive recompute updated every already-closed convocatoria’s integrity_records with the corrected data. A second live test, same convocatoria, returned a genuine, correct “Integrity verified”.
Corrections to public-facing text made possible by this fix: the report UI (4 locales) and the Methodology (§6, v1.2 → v1.3) previously described integrity version 2 as independently verifiable “in a future version” — corrected to describe it as working, now that it demonstrably is, with the one remaining, genuinely unbuilt guarantee (external anchoring — a certified timestamp or public ledger, protecting the seal even against a compromise of Voice Protest’s own database) named explicitly rather than folded into a vaguer “not yet independent” framing. The integrity hash’s algorithm was also corrected from “HMAC-SHA256” (used correctly elsewhere for the identity pseudonyms, which are keyed) to plain SHA256 — the integrity hash is deliberately unkeyed, specifically so it can be recomputed without any secret.
docs/integrity-log.md, previously unpopulated since its creation, was updated with the two convocatorias with confirmed, verified v2 hashes (Cataluña, Las Llamas). A third (Universidad de Utrecht, closed 7 July) carries an integrity_version 1 hash from an older, pre-v2 mechanism with no corresponding integrity_records entry, and was deliberately left out pending a decision on whether to recompute it under v2.
Documents updated in this session: Methodology v1.2 → v1.3 (§6, and the HMAC correction). quienes-somos.html (4 locales) separately corrected the same day to stop describing Stichting Voice Protest as already constituted — an unrelated overclaim found while adapting the page for LinkedIn outreach, corrected in the same spirit as the rest of this entry: the platform, and this document, say only what is currently true.
